1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Roy TAMWO — Wind and solar in France's regions.
How much have these two sources grown between 2013 and 2024?

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
In 2024, they generated 71.6 TWh.
That's equivalent to 16.2% of annual electricity consumption.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
This percentage doesn't mean a region can meet its own needs.
Electricity moves between regions, and supply doesn't always match demand.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Generation rises from 20.6 to 71.6 TWh between 2013 and 2024.
Compared with consumption, it rises from 4.2% to 16.2%.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:54,000
But this ratio doesn't rise every year.
It reaches 16.7% in 2023, then falls to 16.2% in 2024.

6
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:07,000
In 2024: 28.3% in Hauts-de-France, compared with 1.0% in Île-de-France.
The map helps show these differences between regions.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The filters let you keep just the regions you want to compare.
The ranking, map and key figures all update together.

8
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:31,000
You can switch from percentages to generation in TWh.
Producing a lot doesn't mean a region generates enough to meet its needs.

9
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:43,000
For wind power, Hauts-de-France and Grand Est come out on top.
The chart now shows only electricity generated by wind.

10
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:55,000
For solar power, Nouvelle-Aquitaine and Occitanie take the lead.
The regions have quite different generation profiles.

11
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The period filter changes which years are included.
The map, ranking and key figures use the last year you select.

12
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Wind is shown in blue, and solar in orange.
This chart shows how their generation changes year by year.

13
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:27,000
The study uses 156 rows: 13 regions over 12 years.
No missing values in the fields used, and no duplicate region–year pairs.

14
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The tables show the exact figures.
You can also download the filtered data as a CSV file.

15
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The data comes from RTE, through ODRE and data.gouv.fr.
Downloaded on 20 October 2026, under the French Open Licence 2.0.

16
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Wind and solar have grown, with clear differences between regions.
To understand why, we'd also need weather, equipment and hourly data.
